Lars Kecke wrote on Wed, 15 Oct 2003 20:01:52 +0200:
> Rast wrote:
>> 
>> What is the expected value of the number of wishes from fountains in one
>> game of nethack?  Assume you don't die, and that every sink is turned into a
>> fountain.
>> 

> Let's see. You get 1/10 water daemons per fountain and (20-dlvl)/100
> wishes per daemon. So for n fountains/level (anybody got a good
> approximation here?) you have n*19/100 wishes from randomly generated
> fountains.

Considering also the rogue level and the 2/5 chance of a big room
(let's consider it fountainless for this part of the calculation)
you'd get (approximately, as we are ignoring the shift these special
levels create):

n * 1/100 * (17 + 3/5) = n * 22/125 

> Additionally there are the 4 oracle fountains (2/5 daemons,
> 66/400 wishes/daemon=33/500 wishes) and the 2 minetown fountains (4/30
> daemons each due to dryup warning with approximately (sorry, only
> inserted level 7 here) 13/100 wishes/daemon=13/750).

Let's not consider these fixed fountains (and the bigroom's 4/5
fountains) for now. The real hairiness of the problem comes when
calculating this n..

First we need to know how many rooms per level there are on average
(not trivial). Then, there's a fixed chance of a fountain (or
fountanizeable sink) per room, but only if this room has an empty
square (i.e. not a shop, (throne|lepr) hall, or some other special
room [let's ignore the chance of a regular room being full]). So we
need to take into account all these special rooms and extract them.
They are also level-dependent and this needs to be taken into account
too, not only the total number.

Then we still need to add the fixed fountains correctly... All in
all, this is quite a lot of work, I think :-)

